  "image_name": "5ad8300cc6c17fc0458ea0ac6beb12d9.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how fatty liver disease and alcohol-related liver injury converge on similar damaging pathways in the liver. On the left, common NAFLD risk factors—such as insulin resistance, diabetes, obesity, visceral fat, poor diet, inactivity, and inherited susceptibility—are grouped together with alcohol intake, indicating that both can drive liver injury.\n\nInside the liver, the first visible step is impaired lipid handling, shown as fat accumulation. This appears to lead to oxidative and endoplasmic reticulum stress, cell death, and production of reactive oxygen species (ROS). These injured liver cells then feed into immune activation.\n\nA second major input comes from the intestinal microbiome. The figure highlights NAFLD- and ALD-related microbiome disruption, with increased ethanol production, lipopolysaccharides, and other microbiome-derived products reaching the liver. These signals, together with cellular stress, activate inflammatory cells in the liver.\n\nThe inflammatory response is emphasized by chemokine upregulation, neutrophil-driven immunity, and macrophage activation. Overall, the diagram presents a shared mechanism in which metabolic risk and alcohol, amplified by gut-derived signals, promote liver inflammation and ultimately accelerate fibrosis and disease progression.",
